>>>>> On 28 Aug 92 14:39:44 GMT, dashiell@NADC.NADC.NAVY.MIL (A. Kaniss) said:
dashiell> Right nethack has a shell command.

  This reminds me of an old idea, which would be typically nethackish.
When using the shell-command, there should be some percentage chance
of getting a real shell -- that is, one in the *game*, which you could
a)pply.
  It should only be possible to get a shell once in each game and
after some unsuccessful N random attemts (shelling out) you should
loose your chance of getting it at all, all to prevent misuse
(otherwise you'd have an unlimited source of shells). The overall
chance of getting one should be, say, 10% +/- whatever depending on
whatever (luck, time since last attempted shell-command etc).
  Now, what good could a shell be?  Naturally, you would apply it to
your ear and listen to it.  An uncursed shell might just reveal some
noises from monsters nearby ("Your hear an enchanting snore", when a
nymph is taking a nap around the corner etc) and a blessed shell might
even when applied (i.e. momentarily) convey the positions of monsters,
much like a helmet of telepathy.  Cursed shells, on the other hand,
might do damage to your ears so you (until "Your ears feel better")
don't hear anything -- no mumblings about shoplifters, no water
falling on coins, noone couting money etc.
  So, what do you think?  I think this would definitely add a funny
(and, as I said, very nethackish) twist to the shell command.
Naturally, it should not be documented anywhere ...

